@* Written in each component, not in an _Imports.razor, whose class Razor makes public (ADR-0201).
   Every product using is written with global::. The component lives in ExPivot.Components, where a
   plain "ExPivot.Engine" could be looked up against the ExPivot component class first, and a plain
   "ExGrid.X" against sibling types (the CS0426 trap in AGENTS.md). The Web namespace makes @on...
   handlers compile as event handlers rather than as plain HTML attributes. *@
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web
@using global::ExGrid
@using global::ExGrid.Cells
@using global::ExGrid.Chrome
@using global::ExGrid.Columns
@using global::ExGrid.Components
@using global::ExGrid.Rows
@using global::ExGrid.Selection
@using global::ExPivot.Chrome
@using global::ExPivot.Engine
@* Not the contract (ADR-0201): ExPivot places this frame around Show Details' dialog in its own
   markup, and no Consumer places it. It is public only because Razor makes every component public,
   is hidden from a Consumer's editor, and may change in any version. *@
@attribute [System.ComponentModel.EditorBrowsable(System.ComponentModel.EditorBrowsableState.Never)]
@* ExPivot's frame around Show Details' dialog (ADR-0059): a modal dialog named by the cell, over a
   backdrop, whose content is the Chrome's. Escape closes it from anywhere the keyboard is inside it
   that hears the key — the content's own controls, or the frame itself. An Escape pressed in the
   records' grid never reaches the frame: the grid's capture-phase listener keeps every one, peels
   the grid's own layers with them, and raises its OnLeave for the one it has nothing left to
   dismiss, which closes the dialog too (ADR-0070). While it closes, every key heard inside it is
   ExPivot's to hold for the report, the records' grid passing its own out to here (decided with the
   user, 2026-10-13). The frame claims nothing (data-ex-claims=""), which makes it, holding the
   keyboard, the pivot's own; a key its Close claims reaches the frame's handler in that key's turn,
   through the cascade, as in a menu's frame (ADR-0240 section 6). *@
<div class="ex-pivot-dialog" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-label="@Label" tabindex="-1" data-ex-claims="" @onkeydown="OnKeyDownAsync" @ref="_element">
    <CascadingValue Name="@PivotPopupFrame.KeysName" Value="_keys" IsFixed="true">@ChildContent</CascadingValue>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The dialog's accessible name: the cell's title.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Label { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>Escape was pressed inside it.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnEscape { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Every key heard inside it, in place of <see cref="OnEscape"/>: ExPivot closes the dialog on Escape,
    /// and holds the keys typed while it closes for the report (decided with the user, 2026-10-13).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<KeyboardEventArgs> OnKey { get; set; }

    private ElementReference _element;
    private readonly Func<KeyboardEventArgs, Task> _keys;

    /// <summary>Creates the frame.</summary>
    public PivotDialogFrame() => _keys = OnKeyDownAsync;

    /// <summary>The frame's element, which the report's grid takes the keyboard back from.</summary>
    internal ElementReference Element => _element;

    /// <summary>The Chrome's content.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    private Task OnKeyDownAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e) =>
        OnKey.HasDelegate ? OnKey.InvokeAsync(e) : e.Key == "Escape" ? OnEscape.InvokeAsync() : Task.CompletedTask;
}
